    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d271">

                <pb facs="0771" n="726" xml:id="pg726"/>

                <head>271. Summary of Conclusions of a Special Coordination Committee Meeting<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d271-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            from="1980-05-15T09:00:00-05:00" to="1980-05-15T10:00:00-05:00">May 15,
                            1980, 9–10 a.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>Iran and Afghanistan</item>
                </list>
                <list subtype="participants" type="description">
                    <label>PARTICIPANTS</label>
                    <item><list>
                            <label rendition="#italic">State</label>
                            <item>David Newsom</item>
                            <item>Peter Constable</item>
                            <item>Richard Cooper</item>
                        </list></item>
                    <item><list>
                            <label rendition="#italic">OSD</label>
                            <item>W. Graham Claytor</item>
                            <item>Frank Kramer</item>
                        </list></item>
                    <item><list>
                            <label rendition="#italic">JCS</label>
                            <item>Lt. Gen. John Pustay</item>
                        </list></item>
                    <item><list>
                            <label rendition="#italic">CIA</label>
                            <item>Frank Carlucci</item>
                        </list></item>
                    <item><list>
                            <label rendition="#italic">Treasury</label>
                            <item>Deputy Secretary Robert Carswell</item>
                        </list></item>
                    <item><list>
                            <label rendition="#italic">Justice</label>
                            <item>Attorney General Benjamin Civiletti</item>
                        </list></item>
                    <item><list>
                            <label rendition="#italic">White House</label>
                            <item>Zbigniew Brzezinski</item>
                            <item>David Aaron</item>
                            <item>Lloyd Cutler</item>
                            <item>Henry Owen</item>
                            <item>Ray Jenkins</item>
                            <item>Joseph Onek</item>
                        </list></item>
                    <item><list>
                            <label rendition="#italic">NSC</label>
                            <item>Gary Sick</item>
                            <item>Alfred Friendly</item>
                        </list></item>
                </list>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#italic">SUMMARY OF CONCLUSIONS</hi></p>
                <p>1. <hi rendition="#italic">Afghanistan</hi>. The discussion focused on ways to
                    increase media attention on Soviet activities. CIA is providing cameras to go in with the rebel forces. State will
                    contact major U.S. news organizations to attempt
                    to encourage more U.S. correspondents to cover
                    the story on the ground. The U.S. Consulate in
                    Peshawar can provide assistance to journalists who want to travel with rebel
                    groups. VOA will be instructed by State to
                    increase their interviews with journalists who have been inside Afghanistan,
                    with particular emphasis on foreign reporters such as Indians. ICA will prepare a report on their efforts to
                    publicize the story. The Public Information Committee has been meeting regularly
                    to review material, sanitize intelligence information where possible, and get
                    the material to the press. They have had some success in getting stories out in
                    the past week. CIA will examine the possibility
                    of sanitizing recent SIGINT information on
                    Soviet defeats. The Committee will prepare a paper on the systematic preparation
                    and release of information to the media for discussion at the next SCC
                    <pb facs="0772" n="727" xml:id="pg727"/> meeting.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d271-f2"/>
                    CIA will use its resources to encourage more
                    European correspondents to cover the story on the ground. (S)</p>
                <p>[Omitted here is information unrelated to Afghanistan.]</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
